<h1>Another MaraDNS bugfix</h1>
<h2>September 1, 2026</h2>
Another patch for a very minor security bug in MaraDNS.
<a name="2026-09-01-bug-description"> </a>
<h3>Bug description</h3>
<p>Deadwood, when sending a RFC8482 reply to an ANY query, would
 transmit over the network 19 bytes of uninitialized memory. This is a
 possible (but unlikely) leak of private data. This bug was fixed on
 September 1, 2026, in <a href="https://github.com/samboy/MaraDNS/commit/cd87cfd9141949b7ca62455574726defee9e3ad7">commit cd87cfd9</a>; 
a <a href="https://samboy.github.io/MaraDNS/patches/MaraDNS-rfc8482.patch.txt">patch</a> 
is available, and the Windows 32-bit binary was fixed in <a href="https://github.com/samboy/MaraDNS/commit/0f46565c142377ab415d6c56a0e7ddd488ecc4f6">commit 0f46565c</a>.
<p>An analysis of the packets for an unpatched Deadwood in Ubuntu 26.04
 show no significant information is leaked in those 19 bytes.
<p>This bug does not merit a new MaraDNS release.
<a name="2026-09-01-thoughts-on-ai"> </a>
<h3>Thoughts on AI</h3>
<p>Since this is yet another AI-assisted security report, let me discuss
 my feelings about AI. I am not a big fan of AI:
<ul>
<li>
<p>Environmental impact of countless datacenters being built
</li>
<li>
<p>Sky high memory and storage prices, making computers too expensive
</li>
<li>
<p>Jobs being replaced by AI, causing high unemployment
</li>
<li>
<p>People using LLMs as therapists or even as girl/<![if gt IE 6]>&#8203;<![endif]><wbr>boyfriends
</li>
<li>
<p>Countless security reports to trawl through, most of which are minor
</li>
</ul>
<p>I am proud, however, that no major security hole has been found with
 MaraDNS in the age of AI (knock on wood). It’s things like:
<ul>
<li>
<p>Denial of service of just the DNS-over-TCP part of Deadwood (which is
 disabled by default)
</li>
<li>
<p>A very hard to exploit buffer overflow in a program which hasn’t even
 compiled since 2022
</li>
<li>
<p>19 bytes of unallocated heap data can be transmitted over the
 network. To get any important information from this is nay to
 impossible.
</li>
</ul>
<a name="2026-09-01-how-i-use-ai"> </a>
<h3>How I use AI</h3>
<p>AI isn’t entirely bad: I would rather have AI answer a question for
 me than try and find an answer on Stack Overflow.
<p>Also, it’s nice to be able to generate AI summaries of articles, so I
 can have a local mirror of articles I link to in my blog. Indeed, the
 AI summaries solve a problem AI has created: Since countless web bots
 are now being run to train LLMs, websites have retaliated by blocking a
 large number of IPs. To wit, I need to use a VPN to access one of the
 big archiving websites out there.
<p>That in mind, I can’t count on a web archive making some news article
 I link to in my blog permanent anymore; instead I now make an AI
 summary (to remove copyright from the article; note that this legal
 trick only works when the article is reporting on facts, since facts
 are not protected by copyright) so people can get an overview of the
 news I comment on in my blog.


<p>

